4. Architecture Components
4.1 Load Balancer
Generic Concept

A load balancer receives incoming application requests and distributes them across available application instances or containers.

AWS Example

Application Load Balancer (ALB)

Purpose

It prevents all incoming requests from being sent to a single application container.

For example:

1000 learner requests
        |
        v
   Load Balancer
        |
   +----+----+----+
   |    |    |    |
   v    v    v    v
  C1   C2   C3   C4

The load balancer can route requests to the appropriate application service.

5. Containerized Application Services

The application is divided into three main services:

Course Service
Assessment Service
Progress Service

Each service runs inside containers.

5.1 Course Service

The Course Service handles course-related operations.

Examples:

Display available courses
Display course details
Display lessons
Retrieve course information
Provide references to learning materials

Example flow:

Learner
   |
   v
Load Balancer
   |
   v
Course Service Container
   |
   +----> Cache
   |
   +----> Database
5.2 Assessment Service

The Assessment Service handles examinations and assessments.

Examples:

Start an assessment
Retrieve questions
Submit answers
Evaluate submissions
Store assessment results
Display marks

Example:

Learner
   |
   v
Assessment Service
   |
   v
Questions
   |
   v
Learner submits answers
   |
   v
Assessment Service
   |
   v
Store result

The Assessment Service is expected to experience particularly high traffic during examination periods.

Therefore, it is an important candidate for autoscaling.

5.3 Progress Service

The Progress Service tracks learner activity and progress.

Examples:

Completed lessons
Incomplete lessons
Completed assessments
Assessment scores
Overall learning progress

Example:

Learner completes lesson
          |
          v
    Progress Service
          |
          v
     Database
          |
          v
Progress updated
6. Containers

A container packages an application together with the runtime and libraries required to run it.

For example:

+--------------------------------+
|       Course Service           |
|                                |
| Application Code               |
| Runtime                        |
| Required Libraries             |
| Configuration                  |
+--------------------------------+

The container does not contain the application's main database or large learning files.

Those are external cloud resources.

Container
    |
    +------> Managed Database
    |
    +------> Cache
    |
    +------> Object Storage
AWS Examples
Container orchestration: Amazon ECS
Container compute: AWS Fargate
Container image registry: Amazon ECR
7. Container Orchestration

Container orchestration manages the application's containers.

AWS Example

Amazon ECS

ECS can manage multiple instances of each application service.

Example:

ECS


Course Service
    |
    +-- Container 1
    +-- Container 2


Assessment Service
    |
    +-- Container 1
    +-- Container 2


Progress Service
    |
    +-- Container 1
    +-- Container 2

If a container fails, the orchestration platform can help maintain the desired number of running containers.

8. Container Compute

Containers require computing resources such as:

CPU
Memory
Networking
AWS Example

AWS Fargate

Fargate provides the compute capacity required to run the containers without requiring the team to manage the underlying servers directly.

The relationship is:

ECR
 |
 | Container Image
 v
ECS
 |
 | Manages containers
 v
Fargate
 |
 | Provides compute
 v
Running Containers
9. Managed Database

The platform needs structured application data.

Examples include:

Course information
Lesson information
Assessment information
Questions
Student submissions
Marks
Learning progress
Generic Concept

Managed relational database.

AWS Example

Amazon RDS

The project uses one managed relational database rather than creating separate databases for every service.

Conceptually:

                 Managed Database
                       |
       +---------------+----------------+
       |               |                |
       v               v                v
    Courses       Assessments       Progress
    Questions     Submissions       Marks

The database remains the primary source of truth for structured application data.

10. Object Storage

Learning platforms contain large files such as:

Videos
Audio
PDFs
Images
Course materials

These should be stored separately from the relational database.

Generic Concept

Object storage.

AWS Example

Amazon S3

S3
 |
 +-- Videos
 |
 +-- Audio
 |
 +-- PDFs
 |
 +-- Course Materials

The database can store information about the material, while S3 stores the actual files.

For example:

Database:


Lesson 1
Video = lesson1.mp4


S3:


lesson1.mp4
11. Cache

A cache stores frequently accessed information temporarily so that it can be retrieved faster.

AWS Example

Amazon ElastiCache

Example:

Course Service
      |
      v
    Cache
      |
      +---- Data available
      |        |
      |        v
      |      Return data
      |
      +---- Data unavailable
               |
               v
            Database

The cache reduces repeated database reads for frequently requested information.

The database remains the source of truth.

12. Autoscaling

Autoscaling adjusts application capacity according to workload.

Normal period
Assessment Service


Container 1
Container 2
Exam period

Traffic increases significantly:

Learner traffic
      |
      v
Workload increases
      |
      v
Autoscaling
      |
      v
More containers

For example:

2 containers
      |
      v
10 containers

After the exam period:

Traffic decreases
      |
      v
Fewer containers required
      |
      v
10 containers
      |
      v
2 containers
AWS Example

ECS Service Auto Scaling

The important benefit is that capacity can follow demand instead of remaining permanently at peak capacity.

13. Observability

Observability provides visibility into the behavior and health of the system.

It uses three important types of information:

Logs

Records of events that happened.

Example:

Assessment submitted successfully
Database connection failed
User authentication failed
Metrics

Numerical measurements.

Examples:

CPU usage
Memory usage
Request count
Error rate
Response time
Traces

Follow an individual request through multiple components.

Example:

Learner
   |
   v
Load Balancer
   |
   v
Assessment Service
   |
   v
Cache
   |
   v
Database

A trace can help identify which part of the request is taking the most time.

AWS Example

Amazon CloudWatch

CloudWatch provides monitoring capabilities for the AWS environment and application workloads.

14. Infrastructure as Code

Infrastructure as Code means defining infrastructure using code rather than manually configuring every resource.

Tool Example

Terraform

Terraform can describe resources such as:

Load Balancer
Containers
Database
Storage
Networking
Monitoring

Conceptually:

Terraform
    |
    v
Infrastructure Definition
    |
    v
AWS
    |
    +-- Load Balancer
    +-- ECS
    +-- RDS
    +-- S3
    +-- Monitoring
Benefits
Repeatability
Consistency
Automation
Easier infrastructure management
Reduced manual configuration
15. Cost Management and FinOps

Cloud resources have costs associated with their usage.

The platform therefore needs to manage cloud spending.

Basic practices
Scale resources according to demand
Avoid unused resources
Monitor resource usage
Choose appropriate resource sizes
Review storage usage
Monitor cloud spending
Remove unnecessary resources
Example

During normal periods:

Low demand
   |
   v
Fewer containers
   |
   v
Lower compute usage

During exams:

High demand
   |
   v
More containers
   |
   v
Higher capacity
   |
   v
Better learner experience

After exams:

Demand decreases
   |
   v
Scale down
   |
   v
Reduce unnecessary cost
AWS Examples
AWS Cost Explorer
AWS Budgets
AWS cost and usage information

FinOps balances:

Cost
 +
Performance
 +
Reliability
16. Scenario 1 — Normal Course Access

A learner opens a course.

Learner
   |
   v
Load Balancer
   |
   v
Course Service Container
   |
   v
Cache

If the course information is already cached:

Cache
  |
  v
Course information
  |
  v
Learner

If it is not cached:

Cache
  |
  | Cache miss
  v
Database
  |
  v
Course information

The Course Service then returns the information to the learner.

17. Scenario 2 — Learner Watches a Video

The learner selects a lesson video.

