19. Scenario 4 — Exam Period

This is the most important scalability scenario.

Suppose normally:

Assessment Service
2 containers

During an exam:

Thousands of learners
        |
        v
Request volume increases
        |
        v
Assessment Service workload increases
        |
        v
Autoscaling
        |
        v
More containers

For example:

2 containers
     ↓
4 containers
     ↓
8 containers
     ↓
10 containers

The load balancer distributes incoming requests across the available containers.

After the exam:

Traffic decreases
        |
        v
Autoscaling
        |
        v
Containers decrease

This improves both performance and cost efficiency.

20. Scenario 5 — New Course Launch

A new course is launched.

Thousands of learners may request the course at the same time.

Large number of learners
          |
          v
    Load Balancer
          |
          v
    Course Service
          |
          v
       Cache

Frequently requested course information can be served from the cache rather than repeatedly querying the database.

If application workload increases significantly, the Course Service can also scale its containers.

This prevents a sudden increase in traffic from unnecessarily overwhelming one application instance or repeatedly hitting the database for the same information.

21. Scenario 6 — Service Failure

Suppose one Course Service container fails.

Course Service


Container 1  ✓
Container 2  ✗
Container 3  ✓

The orchestration system can maintain the desired number of running containers by replacing the failed container.

The load balancer can continue routing traffic to available healthy containers.

             Load Balancer
              /          \
             v            v
        Container 1   Container 3
             ✓            ✓

This supports application availability.

22. Scenario 7 — Application Becomes Slow

Suppose learners report:

"The assessment is taking too long."

The operations team investigates using observability.

CloudWatch
    |
    +-- Logs
    +-- Metrics
    +-- Traces

They might discover:

Assessment Service
       |
       v
Database
       |
       v
High database usage

Or they might discover:

Assessment Service
       |
       v
High CPU usage

The important point is that observability helps the team identify where the problem is instead of guessing.

23. Scenario 8 — Low Activity and Cost Control

Suppose the platform has very little traffic during a particular period.

Without scaling:

Maximum capacity
       |
       v
Resources remain running
       |
       v
Paying for unused capacity

With autoscaling:

Low demand
    |
    v
Reduce application capacity
    |
    v
Lower resource usage

FinOps then helps the team review:

Resource utilization
Cloud spending
Storage usage
Unused resources
Cost trends

The objective is to avoid unnecessary spending without compromising reliability.

24. Scenario 9 — Infrastructure Reproduction

Suppose the company needs another environment.

For example:

Development
Testing
Production

Instead of manually creating every resource again:

Terraform
    |
    v
Infrastructure definition
    |
    v
Create consistent environment

This demonstrates the purpose of Infrastructure as Code:

Infrastructure becomes repeatable, consistent, and easier to manage.

